<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turin King List</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Turin Royal Canon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> King List, or Turin Royal Canon, is an Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> written in hieratic in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and now in the Museo Egizio in Turin. It lists gods and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with their reign lengths, including many minor rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a> omitted from other lists. Badly fragmented, it has been reconstructed by modern scholars, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-ryholt">Kim Ryholt</a>, whose work identified the Sixteenth and Abydos dynasties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turin-king-list.jpg" alt="Turin King List" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_cosiddetto_%27Papiro_dei_Re_o_Liste_dei_Re%27_2_C1874vs_3.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
